        ":question/id": "0b547b24-9888-53eb-ab95-e124d3f31432",
        ":question/problem": "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = \\cos^{3} 4x$.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=u^3,\\,u=\\cos v,\\,v=4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=3u^2,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}=-\\sin v,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\\n&=3u^2\\cdot (-\\sin v)\\cdot 4\\\\\n&=-12\\cos^{2} 4x\\sin 4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      "role": "independently authored inventory",
      "relationship_to_source_templates": "Substantive captured legacy generator derivative exercise using the same trigonometric composition and derivative stages; kept separate from MA source evidence and newly authored draft previews."
